#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 1020.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162852} :activity/id #uuid "91237c92-d7c1-506c-95d7-177c7680008d" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048300 :topic/id #uuid "927333d2-5d1a-53f3-be91-292cad443566" :topic/math-academy-id 3122} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162852 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163227 :tutorial/content "Suppose we are given the quadratic form \n\n$$\nQ(\\mathbf{x})= x_1^2-8x_1x_2 + x_2^2\n$$\n\n and the matrix ![](images/13/13fad01fd9fcf327f8c45afa8264d92d9de1db135b7a87add4e43b9617574d8d.svg) that defines  the **change of variable**\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x} =P \\mathbf{y} ,\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere ![](images/c6/c6f0e1bf4282bf2ffedc462005ea30ad8bbe07917bab42eaf7fedf8fd2c49ef7.svg) and ![](images/ad/ad7dec0dac6e2a50d428c7d7dbeda4e71812df2f6ac2d80d6d834ffc82f4a0cb.svg) \n\n How do we express $Q(\\mathbf{x})$ in terms of the components of $\\mathbf{y}?$\n\nFirst, notice that\n\n![](images/ae/aefd4438342fcc5201fd2a3a04e4419f9c5189c4cd103eb63fa1ab6d464edc35.svg) \n\ngives the following expressions for $x_1$ and $x_2\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =2{y}_{1}-{y}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2} & =-{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, if we substitute these into our quadratic form expression, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & ={x}_{1}^{2}-8{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & =(2{y}_{1}-{y}_{2})^{2}+8(2{y}_{1}-{y}_{2}){y}_{2}+(-{y}_{2})^{2} \\\\  & =4{y}_{1}^{2}-4{y}_{1}{y}_{2}+{y}_{2}^{2}+16{y}_{1}{y}_{2}-8{y}_{2}^{2}+{y}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & =4{y}_{1}^{2}+12{y}_{1}{y}_{2}-6{y}_{2}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "129771b6-7bea-5c9d-b4c7-05ef8a7493b2" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4885 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "b50d538b-7a37-5a5b-af5f-d7d425017761" :step/math-academy-id 30536 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162873}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162873 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161991 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161783 :question/id #uuid "248c9a29-6698-5c8c-b674-0b0dc537edf0" :question/math-academy-id "e-8177"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f203c9bf-7d12-56db-aafe-6639162eae93" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048826} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050407}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162601} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162617} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272174} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272175} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357664} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357668} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357674} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492057}] :knowledge-point/title "Changing Variables in a Quadratic Form Given Old Variables Through New Variables"} :step/id #uuid "865b4c88-60a2-55e6-b4b2-f027d0718ebe" :step/math-academy-id 30612 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162874}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162874 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161992 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161784 :question/id #uuid "1c199cdf-ca23-53e8-8b5d-1ba43c842d41" :question/math-academy-id "e-8178"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9f2a5391-d8bb-5084-941a-bb787e8729f3" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162602} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162621} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272176} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357678} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357680} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357683} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357684} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375965} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378018} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492009} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492048}] :knowledge-point/title "Changing Variables in a Quadratic Form Given New Variables Through Old Variables"} :step/id #uuid "124e7230-af5a-57e2-95dc-9c539b7d58db" :step/math-academy-id 30613 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162875}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162875 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163236 :tutorial/content "Suppose we are given a quadratic form \n\n$$\nQ(\\mathbf{x})= \\mathbf{x}^T \\! A\\mathbf{x}\n$$\n\n \n\nand an invertible matrix $P$ that defines  the change of variable\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x} =P \\mathbf{y}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nIf this change of variable is applied to the quadratic form $Q(\\mathbf{x}),$ what will be the new matrix of $Q$?\n\nBy making the necessary substitutions, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & ={x}^{T}\\,Ax \\\\  & =(Py)^{T}\\,A\\,(Py) \\\\  & ={y}^{T}{P}^{T}\\,AP\\,y \\\\  & ={y}^{T}({P}^{T}AP)y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,  the new matrix of our quadratic form is \n\n$$\nB=  P^T \\! A P.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "61f41743-ca11-557a-b0c1-bba72921428a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4901 :tutorial/title "The Connection Between Matrices of Quadratic Forms in Different Bases"} :step/id #uuid "5900140f-95ae-5f3f-85f7-2351c6ab39d0" :step/math-academy-id 30614 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162876}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162876 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161993 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161785 :question/id #uuid "4c239ba2-66c9-5170-8a5a-9f7071315dd2" :question/math-academy-id "e-8179"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "331cb610-6168-5161-b821-237c713dedaa" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162603} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162622} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272177} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357685} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357686} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357687} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378025} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394908} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394915} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394922} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394929}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Matrix of a Quadratic Form Given a Change-of-Coordinates Matrix"} :step/id #uuid "d3a1f8da-11aa-5561-b435-f00a5790a026" :step/math-academy-id 30615 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162877}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162877 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163237 :tutorial/content "Two quadratic forms $Q_1$ and $Q_2$ on $\\Bbb R^n$ are said to be **equivalent** if they can be obtained from each other through a change of variable.\n\n*The two quadratic forms with matrices $A$ and $B$ are equivalent if and only if there exists an invertible matrix $P$ such that* \n\n$$\nB = P^T \\! A \\, P.\n$$\n\n**Note:** Equivalent quadratic forms represent the same function from  $\\Bbb R^n$ to $\\Bbb R.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "abe7e02c-7997-5e41-9c51-98c6291198a1" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4902 :tutorial/title "Equivalent Quadratic Forms"} :step/id #uuid "75121285-4880-5444-b03b-36d42d881cfe" :step/math-academy-id 30616}] :activity/title "Change of Variables in Quadratic Forms" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186162218}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046658} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503828} :activity/id #uuid "e8bff054-dda8-555c-88cb-c434ecb5f2f5" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048300 :topic/id #uuid "927333d2-5d1a-53f3-be91-292cad443566" :topic/math-academy-id 3122} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503773 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357686 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03a71052-5e5a-507b-8bda-885193362564" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}7 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -14\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354686} {:answer/id #uuid "2be3bb07-6365-5037-b4b3-e11820f84cac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355037} {:answer/id #uuid "79c0a991-6850-5e2c-a589-9eb8a602cba2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}5 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355731} {:answer/id #uuid "896fa9c9-4232-51c5-8120-eb24b94e0ea9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355881} {:answer/id #uuid "ec65c1ea-cb5e-52c5-b6ab-41489c7848b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356726}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354686} :answer-field/id #uuid "7a379213-9c34-54c6-a8c5-7b300d5ad1d8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357133}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "e3dc7c55-f986-54b3-95d7-1e0702bd8e87" :question/math-academy-id "q-94185" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices $A$ and $P$ above. Find the matrix of the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,Ax$ in the basis that consists of the columns of $P.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $x=[{x}_{1},{x}_{2}]^{T}$ is a vector given by its coordinates in the standard basis and $y=[{y}_{1},{y}_{2}]^{T}$ is the same vector but given in the coordinates relative to the basis that consists of the columns of $P,$ then \n\n$$\nx=Py.\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{T}\\,Ax & =(Py)^{T}\\,A\\,(Py) \\\\  & =({y}^{T}{P}^{T})\\,A\\,(Py) \\\\  & ={y}^{T}({P}^{T}\\,AP)\\,y,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that the matrix of our form in the new basis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{T}\\,AP & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 5 \\\\ 5 & -4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}7 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -14\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "2356820b-ea13-5b1f-9c6d-71a0eb74b5d9" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503832}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503776 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357680 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03a67e30-680d-511e-bfe1-bea257f28b03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-12{y}_{2}^{2}-2{y}_{1}{y}_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354685} {:answer/id #uuid "0c1edce9-c370-5e40-b12f-1849a61198a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-12{y}_{1}^{2}-4{y}_{1}{y}_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354775} {:answer/id #uuid "ae0de4f9-f14a-5370-9df7-aef8e3b8fa29" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-11{y}_{2}^{2}-4{y}_{1}{y}_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356199} {:answer/id #uuid "b4643982-e229-50af-bf2a-dd5b01412401" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8{y}_{2}^{2}-8{y}_{1}{y}_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356248} {:answer/id #uuid "d56fbb6c-5c63-5086-9cc6-6a7d2296760f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-9{y}_{1}^{2}-4{y}_{1}{y}_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356533}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186356199} :answer-field/id #uuid "b006da88-8355-5016-92f4-c470ca280238" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357239}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "e6bf1347-e670-5b02-863d-51c6f44a9ebc" :question/math-academy-id "q-94171" :question/problem "Given that \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\n express the quadratic form $Q(x)={x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}$ in terms of the components of $y.$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\ngives the following expressions for ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & =3{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{2} & =-{x}_{1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy solving the second equation for ${x}_{1}$ and substituting it into the first equation, we obtain the following expressions for ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =-{y}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2} & ={y}_{1}+3{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute these into our quadratic form expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & ={x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{1}{x}_{2} \\\\  & =(-{y}_{2})^{2}+4(-{y}_{2})({y}_{1}+3{y}_{2}) \\\\  & ={y}_{2}^{2}-4{y}_{1}{y}_{2}-12{y}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & =-11{y}_{2}^{2}-4{y}_{1}{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "364480f3-7505-5086-9537-4c177a2685d4" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503798}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503798 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394915 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c98ff09-427a-5eb1-a200-d23ed4191cea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394917} {:answer/id #uuid "9348f60d-beec-50bd-adf0-24bfc6cb780e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394918} {:answer/id #uuid "cc5670d6-74a3-5137-b88e-c8ccd60a7bac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394919} {:answer/id #uuid "2bc8e19b-3088-56cf-9a10-9aa2d5b034de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394920} {:answer/id #uuid "a66b36c9-3e25-5639-a868-08d8ac845797" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394921}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394918} :answer-field/id #uuid "7a6d844e-734f-5b7d-8c23-1672424ae4ba" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394916}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "765a6f68-9c89-5828-8b01-5ec72f2b19e0" :question/math-academy-id "q-94184" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices $A$ and $P$ above. Find the matrix of the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,Ax$ in the basis that consists of the columns of $P.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $x=[{x}_{1},{x}_{2}]^{T}$ is a vector given by its coordinates in the standard basis and $y=[{y}_{1},{y}_{2}]^{T}$ is the same vector but given in the coordinates relative to the basis that consists of the columns of $P,$ then \n\n$$\nx=Py.\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{T}\\,Ax & =(Py)^{T}\\,A\\,(Py) \\\\  & =({y}^{T}{P}^{T})\\,A\\,(Py) \\\\  & ={y}^{T}({P}^{T}\\,AP)\\,y,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that the matrix of our form in the new basis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{T}\\,AP & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "78a09b4f-e453-581b-87df-00053d9228ff" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503773}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503828 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162603 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "14a287e1-30cf-5179-8970-afcf49d2f5cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}5 & 13 \\\\ 13 & 14\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394875} {:answer/id #uuid "87d59086-7fec-5d3e-af93-3090d245ddc2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}5 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394876} {:answer/id #uuid "f54938d5-0c62-555d-8cc1-ab094bc352d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394877} {:answer/id #uuid "e24069dc-e91b-55a8-8298-875171f474a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}5 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 17\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394878} {:answer/id #uuid "65ce5b0c-4582-517a-abb7-afa64972ba57" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394879}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394875} :answer-field/id #uuid "9e354856-dfba-5b4c-b973-04f47aaf9d4a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394874}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "1981982e-98dc-5f26-a882-3a23d659ddac" :question/math-academy-id "q-93738" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices $A$ and $P$ above. Find the matrix of the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,Ax$ in the basis that consists of the columns of $P.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $x=[{x}_{1},{x}_{2}]^{T}$ is a vector given by its coordinates in the standard basis and $y=[{y}_{1},{y}_{2}]^{T}$ is the same vector but given in the coordinates relative to the basis that consists of the columns of $P,$ then \n\n$$\nx=Py.\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{T}\\,Ax & =(Py)^{T}\\,A\\,(Py) \\\\  & =({y}^{T}{P}^{T})\\,A\\,(Py) \\\\  & ={y}^{T}({P}^{T}\\,AP)\\,y,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that the matrix of our form in the new basis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{T}\\,AP & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 6 \\\\ 4 & 9\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 13 \\\\ 13 & 14\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "d4152a7a-e248-52d2-ad02-b7b482c5a91e" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503776}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503832 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272176 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01bcee7d-c18b-5290-b860-ece3e5782254" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{y}_{1}^{2}+5{y}_{1}{y}_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269279} {:answer/id #uuid "39c6bc20-9e27-5b1d-b18b-f5ab734b0894" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{y}_{1}^{2}+4{y}_{1}{y}_{2}-8{y}_{2}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269794} {:answer/id #uuid "640c0d02-7517-5fa1-99a0-2d4f419ec5d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-{y}_{1}^{2}+3{y}_{1}{y}_{2}+8{y}_{2}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270157} {:answer/id #uuid "771b0292-67c0-5132-876e-28a8b44bb8b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-{y}_{1}^{2}+3{y}_{1}{y}_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270322} {:answer/id #uuid "e93ca6ed-0462-567a-8ce1-5c1a5fdcf884" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{y}_{1}^{2}-4{y}_{1}{y}_{2}+4{y}_{2}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271308}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186270322} :answer-field/id #uuid "756b282c-1872-55ab-a930-f41e2431e417" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271718}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "5fc6da69-d546-5839-88d5-382690439002" :question/math-academy-id "q-140939" :question/problem "Given that \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\n express the quadratic form $Q(x)=2{x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{1}{x}_{2}-{x}_{2}^{2}$ in terms of the components of $y.$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\ngives the following expressions for ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & =-2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{2} & =-{x}_{1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy solving the second equation for ${x}_{1}$ and substituting it into the first equation, we obtain the following expressions for ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =-{y}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2} & ={y}_{1}-2{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute these into our quadratic form expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & =2{x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{1}{x}_{2}-{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & =2(-{y}_{2})^{2}+(-{y}_{2})({y}_{1}-2{y}_{2})-({y}_{1}-2{y}_{2})^{2} \\\\  & =2{y}_{2}^{2}-{y}_{2}{y}_{1}+2{y}_{2}^{2}-{y}_{1}^{2}+4{y}_{1}{y}_{2}-4{y}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & =-{y}_{1}^{2}+3{y}_{1}{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "e80e0650-b373-5c35-baf3-e32645bedb71"}] :activity/title "Change of Variables in Quadratic Forms" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186502977}]}
